1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:08,333
The brush uncovers a drum of sandstone, and the surface is extraordinary — polished to a mirror, holding the lamplight like glass.

2
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:14,255
It is the signature finish of Mauryan stonework.

3
00:00:14,255 --> 00:00:20,401
An empire that can do this to stone can do other difficult things too, and this episode is about those.

4
00:00:23,267 --> 00:00:24,176
Four sources.

5
00:00:24,176 --> 00:00:26,125
The Arthashastra, for theory.

6
00:00:26,125 --> 00:00:33,984
Megasthenes' Indica, for an outsider's description — though it survives only as fragments quoted by later Greek writers,

7
00:00:33,984 --> 00:00:35,803
which is a real limitation.

8
00:00:35,803 --> 00:00:39,505
The edicts, for administration in the ruler's own words.

9
00:00:39,505 --> 00:00:42,728
And excavation at Pataliputra and elsewhere.

10
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:46,131
Start with the shape of it.

11
00:00:46,131 --> 00:00:52,409
The empire was governed from Pataliputra, with provinces under governors who were often princes of the royal family.

12
00:01:04,367 --> 00:01:08,993
Four provincial capitals are usually named: Taxila in the north-west,

13
00:01:08,993 --> 00:01:13,620
Ujjain in the west, Suvarnagiri in the south, and Tosali in the east.

14
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:18,048
Below them, districts and villages, with officers the edicts name:

15
00:01:18,048 --> 00:01:23,371
rajukas, who had authority over large rural areas, pradeshikas, and yuktas.

16
00:01:23,633 --> 00:01:27,089
Megasthenes describes Pataliputra as a great city on the river,

17
00:01:27,089 --> 00:01:34,486
defended by a timber palisade with hundreds of towers and many gates — and excavation has in fact found massive wooden palisade remains,

18
00:01:34,486 --> 00:01:38,134
which is a satisfying case of a text and the ground agreeing.

19
00:01:40,167 --> 00:01:48,047
He describes its administration by committees handling crafts, foreigners, births and deaths, trade, manufactures and tax collection.

20
00:01:48,047 --> 00:01:54,229
How literally to take his numbers is debated; the structure is plausible, the detail is secondhand.

21
00:01:57,133 --> 00:01:58,106
And the money.

22
00:01:58,106 --> 00:02:06,407
Land revenue was the backbone, a share of the produce, assessed and collected by a salaried bureaucracy rather than farmed out.

23
00:02:06,407 --> 00:02:10,144
The state ran mines, forests and workshops directly.

24
00:02:11,567 --> 00:02:16,525
It invested in irrigation: the Sudarshana lake in Saurashtra was built under the Mauryas,

25
00:02:16,525 --> 00:02:20,217
and we know that because a later inscription records repairing it.

26
00:02:20,217 --> 00:02:23,688
A standing army and a large officialdom were paid out of this,

27
00:02:23,688 --> 00:02:28,396
which is exactly what the Nandas had shown was possible, on a far bigger scale.

28
00:02:30,633 --> 00:02:35,079
After Ashoka's death about 232 BCE, the empire shrinks quickly.

29
00:02:35,079 --> 00:02:37,880
The later Mauryas are barely known.

30
00:02:39,433 --> 00:02:47,451
The last, Brihadratha, was killed about 185 BCE by his own commander-in-chief, Pushyamitra Shunga, who founded the next dynasty.

31
00:02:47,451 --> 00:02:53,717
From the first unification of the subcontinent to the end took about a hundred and forty years.

32
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:55,073
Why did it fall?

33
00:02:55,073 --> 00:02:56,651
The explanations divide.

34
00:02:56,651 --> 00:03:04,353
One group of arguments is economic and administrative: an empire this centralised needed constant revenue and able kings,

35
00:03:04,353 --> 00:03:09,719
and had neither for long; the provinces had no independent strength to fall back on.

36
00:03:09,719 --> 00:03:15,195
Another is political: weak successors, revolts, and pressure from the north-west.

37
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:25,498
An older view blamed Ashoka's pacifism and his patronage of Buddhism for weakening the state — that one is now largely rejected,

38
00:03:25,498 --> 00:03:31,099
because the empire outlasted him by half a century and the edicts show no dismantling of the army.

39
00:03:31,099 --> 00:03:33,775
Say which explanation you prefer and why.

40
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:41,110
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

41
00:03:41,110 --> 00:03:47,831
Taxila, Ujjain, Suvarnagiri, Tosali — four provinces, one capital at Pataliputra.

42
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:51,108
Dig Quiz.

43
00:03:51,108 --> 00:03:52,643
Pause and name it.

44
00:03:52,643 --> 00:03:58,300
First: the officers the edicts give authority over large rural areas.

45
00:03:58,300 --> 00:03:59,650
The rajukas.

46
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:06,697
Second: the irrigation work in Saurashtra built under the Mauryas and repaired by later rulers.

47
00:04:06,697 --> 00:04:08,030
The Sudarshana lake.

48
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:15,062
Third: the Greek ambassador's account of India, which survives only as fragments quoted by later writers.

49
00:04:15,333 --> 00:04:16,185
The Indica.

50
00:04:16,185 --> 00:04:21,314
In Mains, a frequent question asks why the Mauryan empire declined.

51
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:32,679
Set out the administrative, economic and political explanations,

52
00:04:32,679 --> 00:04:38,129
say which you find strongest, and explain why the pacifism argument is no longer accepted.

53
00:04:39,867 --> 00:04:44,800
A polished pillar section takes its place, and the fourth room of the gallery is full.

54
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,794
Next gallery:

55
00:04:46,794 --> 00:04:53,885
the subcontinent breaks into pieces again — and some of the most interesting things in ancient India happen in those pieces.

56
00:04:53,885 --> 00:04:57,418
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

